

⭐ UI Shell — The CMB Cinematic Cockpit

This is the interface layer that sits on top of:

the Media Engine

the Data Channel

the Overlay Engine

the Sync Controller

It’s the part you touch, the part you navigate, the part that feels like a control surface for your universe.

⭐ 1. Layout Overview

The UI shell is divided into four primary surfaces:

Playback Bar — time navigation

Overlay Panel — toggle layers

Blueprint Panel — show metadata

Viewport — the cinematic canvas

Below is the structure:

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                       TOP TOOLBAR                             │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  OVERLAY PANEL   │                VIEWPORT                    │
│                  │                                            │
│                  │                                            │
│                  │                                            │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                       PLAYBACK BAR                            │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

⭐ 2. Top Toolbar

This is the command strip.

Load File (local or GitHub URL)

Load Data (Θ, Ξ, organisms, metrics)

Snapshot (export a frame)

Mode Switch (Cinematic / Scientific / Blueprint)

Settings (performance, color maps, grid density)

Each button is tied to a viewer action, not a file action.

⭐ 3. Overlay Panel

This is your layer control center.

Each overlay is a toggleable item:

Time Field Θ

Meta‑Model Field Ξ

Organisms

Metric Grid

Time Dilation Map

Blueprint HUD

Each item has:

checkbox

opacity slider

color map selector

detail level selector

This is how you see inside the simulation.

⭐ 4. Viewport (Cinematic Canvas)

This is the heart of the viewer.

It displays:

the cinematic video

the overlay layers

the HUD

the blueprint metadata (optional)

It supports:

zoom

pan

fullscreen

frame stepping

overlay blending

This is the window into your universe.

⭐ 5. Playback Bar

This is your time navigation strip.

Play / Pause

Frame Step (forward/back)

Scrubber

Current Time / Total Time

Playback Speed (0.25× → 4×)

Loop Region (A/B markers)

It also shows regime markers:

Classical region

Warp region

Frozen region

Organism events

Meta‑equation transitions

These appear as colored ticks on the timeline.

⭐ 6. Blueprint Panel

This is where the viewer becomes more than a video player.

It shows:

the blueprint snippet that generated the scene

the active time model

the meta‑equation state

organism lifecycle events

field values at the current frame

Example:

model WARP {
  equation: T_obs = ∫ Φ(Θ, g, u) dλ;
  modifies: Θ, Ξ;
}

This panel makes the viewer a simulation intelligence interface.

⭐ 7. Interaction Model

The UI shell supports three modes:

1. Cinematic Mode

Just the video + minimal overlays.

2. Scientific Mode

Full overlays, field maps, metrics.

3. Blueprint Mode

Blueprint panel + HUD + regime markers.

You switch modes from the top toolbar.























